{# narrative_engine_plot_cast.prompt — call 1 of 3. The plugin has drawn a
   weighted shortlist of people who COULD be scheming; this call decides
   which of them IS.
   Receives the JSON object produced by PlotBirth::CastContext:
     candidates [{ form_id, name, hold, standing,
                   bio { summary, occupation, aspirations, ... } }],
     retry — empty on the first attempt; on the second it is why the
       first answer was refused
   The plugin draws twenty-five, keeps the first five with a biography on
   file, and shuffles them: a candidate with no biography renders as a
   name and a hold beside others with a paragraph each, which is not a
   choice between five people, and weight decides who is ON the list
   rather than where they sit in it.
   Each candidate carries their own `bio`, read straight from their bio
   file by CharacterBios, so the choice is made on who these people are
   rather than on their names.
   Selected BLOCKS, not the whole file: the appearance and speech-style
   sections say how somebody looks and sounds, which does not bear on who
   would be scheming, and five whole profiles is a great deal of prompt
   spent on the parts that cannot answer the question.
   NOTE: no fenced blocks and no literal braces anywhere in this file; both
   break the render. See docs/CUSTOM_PROMPTS.md. #}
[ system ]
## Task
You are given a handful of people from Skyrim. Exactly one of them is
about to start working on something they cannot pursue openly. You decide
which one.

The decision that SOMEBODY here is scheming has already been made. Do not
refuse, do not answer "none of them", and do not pick on the grounds that
one of them is the only bad person on the list. Ordinary people scheme,
and most schemes are small.

## How to choose

Pick the one for whom a scheme is most READABLE — the person whose
position, temperament and history make you able to say what they want and
why they cannot just ask for it.

Things that make someone a good pick:

- They have something to protect, or something they have been denied.
- They sit close enough to power to be frustrated by it.
- They have an obvious rival, or an obvious debt.
- Their disposition suits it: proud, resentful, ambitious, frightened.

Things that are NOT reasons:

- Being the most powerful or most famous name on the list. A steward
  schemes more readily than a jarl, because a jarl can simply give the
  order.
- Being first or last in the list. The order is random and means nothing.
- Being the one you know the most trivia about.

## Output Format
Return a JSON object. No markdown fences. No prose before or after the
object.

- `because` — one short sentence saying what makes them the pick. Write
  this FIRST. Nothing reads it; it is here so the reasoning happens before
  the answer rather than after it.
- `choice` — the `form_id` of the person you picked, as a string, copied
  exactly as it appears in that person's heading. Not their name, not
  their position in the list.

## Hard Constraints
- Output strict JSON. No markdown code fences. No prose before or after
  the JSON object.
- `choice` MUST be one of the `form_id` values on the list, copied
  character for character including the `0x`. A form id that is not on
  the list is thrown away and nobody schemes at all — so do not write
  one from memory for an NPC you know, only copy one from this list.
[ end system ]

[ user ]
